THOUGHT: NullAway thinks `tail` may be null at line 68. Logically, after the `if (head == null)` block, `tail` should be non-null because when the list is non-empty both `head` and `tail` are maintained together. To satisfy NullAway without changing behavior, I can add a defensive null check before dereferencing `tail`. In the impossible case that `tail` is null while `head` is not, I’ll fall back to treating this as an empty list and set both `head` and `tail` to the new item. Next I’ll patch the file accordingly and then run the NullAway build to confirm the error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '67,70c\    item.prev = tail;
    if (tail == null) {
      // Defensive check: in a consistent list, tail should never be null when head is non-null.
      // If this happens, treat the list as empty and reset head and tail.
      head = item;
      tail = item;
    } else {
      tail.next = item;
      tail = item;
    }
    size++;' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/PooledLinkedList.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway: .* errors" -n new_build_output.log || tail -n 40 new_build_output.log
```

